faq:
  - q: "What would $30 a day of bitcoin be worth?"
    a: "Computed from bitcoin's real price history, $30 of bitcoin bought every day since January 1, 2017 put in about $105,000 over roughly nine and a half years and accumulated about 11.3 BTC. Its value first crossed $1,000,000 on November 19, 2024 (about $86,400 contributed by then), and at the latest price in the series sits near $715,000 — below the million, in the current drawdown. Change the start date or the daily amount on this page to replay any other version."
  - q: "What is a bitcoin DCA backtest?"
    a: "Dollar-cost averaging (DCA) is buying a fixed dollar amount on a fixed schedule regardless of price. A backtest replays that habit against real historical prices to show what it would have accumulated, what it cost, and what it is worth. This page is a retrospective backtest — the record of what a past daily habit did, not a forecast of what a future one will do."
  - q: "How long was a daily bitcoin buyer underwater?"
    a: "Underwater means the stack is worth less than the total put into it. Averaging in keeps the cost basis low, so a $30/day buyer since January 2017 was underwater only for the first few weeks and never again — even through the deepest stack drawdown, which fell about 73% from its peak while still staying well above the amount contributed. A worse-timed start date makes that underwater stretch longer; the tool lets you see how much."
  - q: "Is this page a prediction of future returns?"
    a: "No. It is retrospective only — it computes what a past habit actually did against real price history, and shows the deep drawdowns along the way. Past results do not predict future ones, and nothing here is a recommendation to buy on any schedule."
