  <section class="dc-prose-section">
    <h2>The record, not a recommendation</h2>
    <p class="dc-prose">Two of the cards above are the story most retellings skip. <strong>Deepest drawdown</strong> is what the stack did from peak to trough &mdash; for the $30/day run, a fall of about 73%, a paper loss most people never actually sit through. And <strong>longest underwater</strong> is the stretch where the stack was worth less than every dollar put in. Averaging in keeps that second number small: the daily buyer above spends only a few weeks underwater across nine years, because each purchase lowers the average cost. That is the quiet case for a boring habit &mdash; not that it dodges the drawdown, but that it is the vehicle most people can actually hold through one. Start a year ago instead and the stretch runs months, not weeks &mdash; the date input above will show you.</p>
    <p class="dc-prose">This page deliberately stops at what the habit <em>did</em>. Why the trend keeps pulling away from intuition &mdash; the exponential-blindness that makes these numbers feel impossible &mdash; belongs to <a href="/the-doubling-ladder.html">the Doubling Ladder</a>. Whether to spread buying out at all, versus deploying a lump sum, belongs to <a href="/wait-or-deploy-now.html">Wait or Deploy Now</a>. And the channel these blind daily buys actually land in &mdash; floor, trend, upper band &mdash; is <a href="/the-power-law.html">the Power Law</a>. This page only answers one question: what did the drip buy.</p>
  </section>
